The history of computing is a long and winding road. It begins with mechanical calculating devices in the nineteenth century, moves through the relay computers of the nineteen thirties and forties, the vacuum tube machines that followed them, the transistor revolution of the nineteen fifties, and the integrated circuit era that made microprocessors possible. Each generation of hardware brought new programming languages, new operating systems, and new ideas about how people and machines should work together. Operating systems grew from simple batch monitors into complex multitasking kernels with virtual memory, file systems, and networking stacks. Compilers evolved from hand written assemblers into optimizing toolchains. High level languages arrived, spread, and diversified. Databases grew from flat files into relational systems, then into distributed stores. The graphical user interface replaced the command line for most users, while the internet connected machines across the world and turned software into a service. Machine learning arrived next, and neural networks moved from research curiosities into everyday tools. The history of computing is not just a story of circuits and code. It is a story of how we think, how we organize knowledge, and how we imagine
